artifact.self_contained_problem:
Let $(S^2, g)$ be the standard two-dimensional sphere endowed with a Riemannian metric $g$, and let $T^*S^2$ be its cotangent bundle with canonical symplectic form. A natural Hamiltonian system on $S^2$ is a function on $T^*S^2$ of the form
$$H(q,p) = \tfrac{1}{2}\, g^{ij}(q)\, p_i p_j + V(q),$$
where $V \colon S^2 \to \mathbb{R}$ is a smooth potential. A function $F \colon T^*S^2 \to \mathbb{R}$ is called a polynomial integral of degree $d$ if $\{H,F\} = 0$ and, in any canonical local coordinates, $F$ is a polynomial of degree $d$ in the momenta $p_i$. The system $H$ is said to be superintegrable if it admits three functionally independent polynomial integrals (one of which may be taken to be $H$ itself).

Construct, or prove the nonexistence of, a natural Hamiltonian system $H = \tfrac{1}{2} g^{ij} p_i p_j + V$ on $S^2$ with $V$ nonconstant, such that:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item $H$ is superintegrable by polynomial integrals all of whose nontrivial members have degree $\geq 3$ in the momenta;
  \item $H$ admits no nontrivial polynomial integral of degree $1$ or degree $2$ (other than constant multiples of $H$ in degree $2$, where applicable).
\end{enumerate}
